Industry overlay

Targeting a specific industry?
The core roadmap gets you through the door anywhere. The difference between "qualified" and "they hired someone else" often lives in industry-specific context — the norms, the compliance floors, the tools assumed fluent in that sector.


Financial Services & FinTech

Banks and fintech companies applying AI to risk, fraud, and underwriting, where model decisions must be explainable and auditable, not just accurate.

Salary premium

+12–22%

Hiring

High

Must have
Model explainability for regulated decisions

Credit and fraud decisions touched by AI must be explainable to regulators and customers under fair lending laws — a black-box model can be a legal liability, not just a technical one.

Must have
Guardrails against hallucinated financial advice

An AI feature that fabricates a balance, rate, or transaction detail creates real financial and legal exposure, so output validation against source-of-truth systems is mandatory.

Must have
Adversarial robustness for fraud models

Fraud rings actively probe AI-based detection systems for blind spots, so engineers must design against active adversaries, not just natural distribution shift.

Important
PII redaction in LLM pipelines

Prompts and logs touching financial AI features routinely contain account numbers and SSNs, requiring redaction before data reaches any third-party model API.

Important
Model risk management (SR 11-7 alignment)

Banks apply a formal model governance framework originally written for statistical models to AI systems, requiring documented validation and ongoing monitoring.

